UqÌ"-<ê¬{"rendered":{"html":"<p>GCSL is a statically typed scripting language. A program can describe a\nconfiguration record, compute a value, or run commands. You use the same\nexpressions for all three, with function types separating pure computation\nfrom operations that can change the outside world.</p>\n<p>The <code>gcsl</code> command runs programs, checks them without execution, formats\nsource files, and serves editor requests. Starting it without a command\nopens an interactive read-evaluate-print loop (REPL).</p>\n<h2 id=\"values-first\">Values first <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#values-first\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A file is a block of declarations and expressions. Named fields form its\nresulting record. This configuration computes one field from another:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>name = \"gcsl\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\narchive = \"{name}-{version}.tar.gz\"\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A file can instead return one expression. Use <code>let</code> for private values that\ndon't become fields of a record. The checker catches incompatible types and\nunresolved names before any part of the program runs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"execution-and-trust\">Execution and trust <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#execution-and-trust\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Pure functions use <code>:</code> before their result type. Impure functions use\n<code>-&gt;</code>; they can run processes, access files, and await deferred work.\nMemoized functions use <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> and reuse results within an execution context.\nA <code>lazy</code> expression delays work until it is awaited. It doesn't start a\nbackground task.</p>\n<p>GCSL's type system is not a sandbox. Running a script gives its process and\nfile operations the permissions of the process running GCSL. Check an\nunfamiliar program with <code>gcsl check</code> before deciding whether to run it.\nChecking does not establish that executing it is safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-to-go-next\">Where to go next <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#where-to-go-next\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Start with <a href=\"/gcsl/getting-started/running-a-first-program\">Running a First Program</a>, then\nread <a href=\"/gcsl/language/values-and-bindings\">Values and Bindings</a>. Use <code>gcsl(1)</code> for the\ncommand interface, <code>gcsl(5)</code> for language rules, and <code>gcsl-stdlib(3)</code> for\nstandard functions.</p>\n<p>Pekit embeds GCSL to provide its own build interface. Its definitions and\ncommands belong to Pekit. The separate <code>tree-sitter-gcsl</code> repository owns\nits editor grammar and queries; the compiler parser in this repository is\nthe authority for whether a GCSL program is accepted.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"values-first","title":"Values first","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"execution-and-trust","title":"Execution and trust","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","title":"Where to go next","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[["gcsl/running-a-first-program",{"Ok":["/gcsl/getting-started/running-a-first-program","Running a First Program"]}],["gcsl/values-and-bindings",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/values-and-bindings","Values and Bindings"]}]],"anchors":[]},"refs":[]}